# DESCRIPTION

The base system is one tree, never a set of packages.
A release is built whole from one commit, and its tree is named by a hash: two builds of the same commit give the same name.
Nothing on a running system changes the tree; an update replaces it whole, so there is no partial state and nothing to resolve.

Three things do the work, each one job:

- the content store (see store(6)) keeps trees as objects named by their BLAKE2b hashes, so a file two releases share is stored once, and every byte read from a tree can be checked against the tree's name;
- the boot slots on the EFI system partition (see slots(6)) each hold one release's kernel and boot image, so booting picks a release, and the release before stays bootable;
- the system volume's snapshots (see storage(7)) keep what a release does not contain, the machine's configuration in `/cfg`, so a rollback can take that back too.

install(8) puts the first release on a disk from an install medium.
distd(8) serves the store as `/dist`, checks releases in it, and applies one to a slot or rolls back to the slot before; both take effect at the next boot.
Nothing updates by itself: every change is a command an administrator writes.

@node=release title="Releases and the store"

A release is a record and the trees it names.
The record, release(6), is ndb text: the release's number, which only grows, its name, channel and commit, and for each architecture the hash of its `base` tree.
`./build release` builds both architectures, puts each base tree into `out/release/store` with vxstore(8), and writes the record beside it, `out/release/release.ndb`; `./build release --verify` builds again and compares the trees' hashes with a record's.

In the store, a file is cut into blocks of 64 KiB, each named by its hash; the file is named by the root of a hash tree over its blocks, with its size, and stored as an index of the block hashes; a directory is ndb text listing its entries' names, modes and hashes, named by the hash of that text.
So the name of the root directory names every byte of the tree, and a reader can check any one block against it without reading the rest of the file.
Only names, contents, links and whether a file is executable go into a tree: no owners and no times, which is what lets two builds agree.

On an installed system the store is the system volume's `store` branch: objects under `b2/`, records under `records/`.
distd reads every record there, at its start and when `ctl` says `rescan`, and serves each release of its own architecture as `/dist/releases/`<n>, its base tree read-only at `/dist/releases/`<n>`/tree`.
Every directory is checked against its hash before it is used, every index against the file's, and every block against the index before a byte of it is returned; a read that does not match fails with an I/O error and is said on the console.
The volume's own block hashes catch failing media; these catch a store that has been changed.

@node=install title="Installing"

An install medium is a disk or CD image whose Limine loads, beside the kernel and the boot image, one more module, `store.tar`: a release's record and every object of its base tree.
Its command line has `vx.live`, so svcd(8) starts the services marked `when=vx.live`, and gives the one whose manifest says `storeimage` the store image.
Since the firmware reads the medium, the installer needs a driver only for the disk it writes to.

install(8) is that service.
It checks the whole tree in the image against its record, then, with `-y`, erases the disk its manifest connects it to and writes:

- a GUID partition table: an EFI system partition, FAT32, 512 MiB unless `-e` says otherwise, and the system volume over the rest;
- slot `a` on the ESP: the release's kernel, the root task's modules and boot image under `\EFI\vectra\a`, Limine at the firmware's fallback path, the slot table, and Limine's configuration, made from the table, naming each file with its BLAKE2b hash, which Limine checks before it loads it;
- the system volume, with its branches `store`, `cfg`, `home` and `adm`, the users `adm`, `none` and `vectra`, and the release's objects and record copied into `store`.

Without `-y` it checks the medium and the disk, says what it would erase, and writes nothing.
The installed slot's command line has `vx.system` in place of `vx.live`, so the next boot from the disk starts the installed system's services: partd(8), fsd(4), dosfs(4) on the ESP, and distd.

@node=slots title="Slots and Limine"

The ESP has three slots, `\EFI\vectra\a`, `b` and `c`, each one release's `kernel.elf`, `svcd`, `ktest` and `bootfs.tar`.
The slot table, `\EFI\vectra\slots.ndb`, says which release each slot holds, with each file's hash, which slot boots, and which booted before it.
Limine's configuration, `\boot\limine\limine.conf`, is made from the table alone, each time the table is written: an entry for each slot in use, the slot that boots as the default, and `vx.slot=`<x> on each entry's command line, so distd knows which slot it booted from.

There is one Limine, at the firmware's fallback path, and no UEFI boot entry: choosing the slot that boots is rewriting the configuration.
The menu lists every slot, so a release that does not come up is left by choosing another slot at boot.

The system booted from a slot runs from that slot's boot image, served by bootfs(4); the release's tree in the store is the same files, checked, but it is what updates copy from, not what the system runs.

@node=apply title="Applying and rolling back"

`apply` <n>, written to `/dist/ctl`, stages release <n>:

- its whole tree is checked, every object present and matching its hash;
- `/cfg` is snapshotted as `cfg@apply-`<n>, through the `adm` branch's `ctl` (see fsd(4));
- the release's four boot files are written to a slot that is not the one running, each checked as it is read from the store, then read back from the ESP and compared with its hash;
- the table is rewritten with that slot booting and the running slot as the previous, and Limine's configuration made again from it.

`rollback` makes the previous slot boot again, and the slot being left the previous.
If the slot being left is the one running, it also rolls `/cfg` back to the snapshot its release's `apply` took, so `/cfg` is as it was before that release was applied.
A rollback of a branch keeps what it leaves as a snapshot, so a `/cfg` changed under the newer release is not lost (see storage(7)).
Neither command reboots.

Each file on the ESP is written whole under a new name and renamed over the old, the table first.
Only an administrator, a member of group 0 in `/adm/users` (see users(6)), may write `/dist/ctl`.

`/dist/status` says how many releases distd found and, booted from a slot, `current=`, the release running, `slot=`, its slot, and `boot=`, the release the next boot is.

@node=admin title="What an administrator does"

To update, today:

- put the new release's objects and record into the store, by copying them into the `store` branch from a medium, merging its `b2/` directories into the store's and its record into `records/`;
- write `rescan` to `/dist/ctl`, and see the release counted in `/dist/status`;
- write `apply` <n>, and reboot;
- if the new release comes up wrong, choose the previous slot in Limine's menu, or, from the new release, write `rollback` and reboot.

The commands are written by a process running as an administrator with `/dist`, and for the copy the `store` branch, in its namespace; the slots test does it from a service whose manifest gives it both.

# EXAMPLES

The slots test, `tests/user/slottest.rc`, run as `adm` with `/dist`, the `store` branch on `/n` and a medium holding release 2 on `/m`, copies release 2 in and applies it on the first boot:

```rc
% prefixes=`{ls /m/b2}
% mkdir -p /n/b2/$prefixes
% for(d in $prefixes) cp /m/b2/$d/* /n/b2/$d/
% cp /m/records/2.ndb /n/records/
% echo rescan >/dist/ctl
% echo apply 2 >/dist/ctl
```

and on the boot of release 2 that follows, rolls back:

```rc
% echo rollback >/dist/ctl
```

What the install, distd and the test say over the four boots, on x86_64:

```text
install: release 1 on the medium, checked: 3989 objects, 208 MiB of files
install: erasing /srv/disk0
install: slot a written; the ESP is FAT32 "VECTRA"
install: done: 1265 objects in the store, release 1 in slot a
distd: 1 releases for x86_64; serving /srv/dist
slottest: stage 0, release 1, slot a
distd: release 2 staged: the next boot is its slot's
distd: 2 releases for x86_64; serving /srv/dist
slottest: stage 1, release 2, slot b
distd: rolled back: the next boot is release 1's slot
distd: 2 releases for x86_64; serving /srv/dist
slottest: stage 2, release 1, slot a
```

